2. The recurring pitch structure
The strongest commonality in this transcript is that most teams follow a recognizable sequence:
Name the problem
ALMA starts with the claim that many liquidity-provider positions are out of range and not earning fees. npmguard opens with the risk of malicious npm packages. DIVE begins with problems in centralized prediction-market oracles.
Introduce the solution in one sentence
The teams give the audience a product identity and a simple explanation. Maki is a DeFi agent in your terminal; ENShell is an on-chain firewall for AI agents; PaintGlobal is a platform for preserving and sharing workshop paintings.
Show a concrete user journey
Most of the demos are not just screenshots. They show a user performing an action, followed by an observable result: a trade executes, a liquidity position is rebalanced, a transaction is signed, a package is audited, or a painting is minted.
Explain the technical innovation
The presenters then describe how the product works and which integrations or mechanisms enable it. The details differ substantially across projects.
Finish with a working result or a clear product benefit
Several presenters end by restating the utility, while others show the final state of the application. PaintGlobal goes a step further by inviting the audience to try the product and contribute their paintings.
