3. What the individual finalists did well—and what to learn
These are observations about the recorded pitches, not claims about why the judges selected these teams.
Défi: Make the product understandable immediately
Clear product framing
The presenter explains the product as a one-on-one trading competition with a friend. They then demonstrate creating a challenge, sharing the link, choosing the trade and watching both players' positions and profit-and-loss figures update in real time.
Takeaway: Give the audience an intuitive mental picture before introducing the technical stack. Here, the social competition is the hook; the integrations and real-time trading are supporting details.
ALMA: Use a quantitative problem statement
Problem-led opening
ALMA begins with a statistic about liquidity positions sitting outside their range and the resulting unproductive capital. It then shows two positions being delegated to its agent, followed by rebalancing and the updated in-range positions.
The presenter also acknowledges that the displayed APY is a placeholder. That's a useful reminder that a live demo should distinguish real outputs from illustrative or unfinished elements.
Takeaway: When you have a credible metric, use it to make the problem concrete. Then demonstrate the specific action your product takes to solve it.
Veil VPN: Demonstrate several differentiators in one journey
Multiple visible proof points
Veil explains the trust problem with conventional VPN providers, then introduces its TEE-based architecture and usage-based payments. In the demo, the presenter connects through an ENS subdomain, shows the changed IP address and displays the balance decreasing as network traffic is used. The presenter also demonstrates a human-verification flow using World ID.
Takeaway: A single user journey can prove multiple product claims if the actions are well chosen. But be careful not to overload the pitch with too many features: each additional feature needs to earn its time.
Maki: Make security visible
Clear technical demonstration
Maki explains that the agent can interact with DeFi from the terminal while keeping private keys on the user's device or hardware wallet. The presenter demonstrates a balance query, an attempted transaction, user confirmation and signing, and a swap.
The first launch fails during the presentation, but the presenter switches to an already running instance and continues with the demo.
Takeaway: Don't let a failed demo setup consume the whole presentation. Have a fallback that still demonstrates the essential working functionality. More importantly, make the security architecture visible through the actual signing flow.
EVM PORST: Lead with a high-stakes technical problem
Technical deep dive
This presenter opens with the threat of quantum computers to current cryptography, then explains the proposed signature scheme, Merkle octopus optimization and gas savings. The demo is a deployed Sepolia contract returning the expected ERC-1271 magic value.
Takeaway: For an infrastructure project, the technical contribution may be the product's core value. The challenge is to make the problem and the improvement comprehensible before the detailed mathematical explanation. The demo should validate the implementation, not merely show source code.
ENShell: Explain the threat model, then show the controls
Problem and interface connected
ENShell opens by explaining how prompt injection, malicious agent-to-agent communication and hallucinations could lead to harmful transactions. It compares this risk to SQL injection and introduces its transaction-analysis and decision-making SDK.
The presenter then shows a live detection interface, the reasons a transaction was blocked, agent threat scores, registry information and a Ledger-based control for killing an agent.
Takeaway: When the product is a security tool, show the threat, the detection and the response. An explanation of the attack alone is not enough; the demo should make the mitigation tangible.
DIVE: Start with a familiar product and expose its limitation
Ambitious problem framing
DIVE uses Polymarket as an example of the problem of relying on a centralized oracle. It then describes a decentralized verification network in which AI agents gather evidence, debate outcomes and develop reputations.
The demo shows agent registration, an explorer with activity and payout information, and a proposed market-dispute flow. Some of the more complex agent interaction and dispute mechanics are explained verbally rather than fully played out in a complete live sequence.
Takeaway: A comparison to an existing product can quickly establish why your solution matters. However, for a complex protocol, be especially selective about which part of the architecture you demonstrate live.
npmguard: Demonstrate a real workflow and explain the underlying system
End-to-end use case
The team opens with a security problem affecting developers, then demonstrates running its CLI, checking dependencies, paying for an audit and installing a package after it passes the audit. The second part of the demo explains its three-stage approach: scanning package files, sandboxed execution and generating a reproducible report.
Takeaway: A compelling technical demo can have two layers: a straightforward user workflow followed by a deeper explanation of the machinery that makes it possible.
Corpus: Show the product as a sequence of decisions
Complex multi-agent workflow
Corpus explains its idea through the analogy of an AI-agent corporation. It introduces the setup of the agents, their operating policies, their work on go-to-market activities, agent-to-agent purchases and human approval for spending above a threshold.
Takeaway: For a complicated product with many interacting components, a demo should follow one coherent scenario from setup to outcome. Otherwise, the audience can get lost in the feature list.
PaintGlobal: Connect the product to the event itself
Contextual storytelling
PaintGlobal begins with the audience's experience of a painting workshop at the hackathon. It explains the problem of forgetting or losing track of the artwork, then shows NFC login, uploading a painting, voting, NFT minting and auctioning.
The presenter ends by inviting attendees to use the application and upload their own paintings. The host subsequently praises the project for connecting the event activity to a full crypto-enabled experience.
Takeaway: A real-world use case that is already happening around you can be a very effective opening. The pitch is easy to follow because the audience has a direct connection to the problem.
