4. The most useful pattern: one complete journey, backed by a technical reason to care
The presentations suggest three distinct ingredients that work together:
The finalist demo formula
1. An understandable problem
Give the judge a reason to care before asking them to understand your technology.
2. A working end-to-end experience
Show one user action, the system's response and a meaningful outcome. Prefer an actual interaction over a slide describing what the product will do.
3. A clear technical differentiator
Show what your implementation makes possible and why the underlying mechanism matters.
There is also an important lesson about polish. In the transcript, several presenters experience errors, pauses or nervousness, yet complete their presentations. That doesn't prove judges overlook polish, but it does show that the finalist presentations were not all flawless performances. The content and demonstration still give us plenty to learn from.
5. What the host's comments add
The closing ceremony host offers some direct, though limited, insight into what he personally found notable:
After Défi, he praises the quick demo and its use of DeFi.
After ALMA, he calls the demonstration quick and useful.
After Veil VPN, he emphasizes the live demonstration and practical consumer utility.
After npmguard, he describes it as a full product ready to use.
After PaintGlobal, he highlights the connection between the painting workshop, NFC, Web3 and audience participation.
These are the host's reactions, not a complete account of the judges' reasoning. The transcript also says judges stayed an additional two hours to talk to teams and review their demos, but does not provide individual judging scores or a detailed explanation of why each finalist was selected. 
